 demanded conclusion: “*You can test whether BAD SUGAR owns any special or irreplaceable pleasure, without denying pleasant taste or giving up your right to choose.*” It defines the destination as freedom “*without deprivation, without battle, and without a lifetime of rules*,” while openly withholding guarantees and clinical claims. The commissioned instruction arrives at the practical climax and removes immediate dread: keep eating normally, begin no new restriction or compensation plan, preserve qualified clinical guidance, and simply observe. The reader ends curious rather than cornered, with the later destination clearly distinguished from either moderation or lifelong discipline.\n\nNo material journey gaps observed.\n\n## Final verdict\n\n**Assigned transition verdict:** The chapter successfully moves the reader from expecting another diet and an endless discipline problem to accepting a low-pressure investigation whose eventual aim is to end the sugar debate altogether. Recognition is specific and nonjudgmental; the contrast between a managed truce and no longer missing BAD SUGAR provides the turn toward a different kind of freedom; and the honest limits prevent the promise from feeling coercive. The instruction is correctly placed after the destination and investigative contract have been established, so continuing to eat normally feels purposeful rather than evasive. Although the real chapter uses much more fear, addiction framing, and immediate certainty, those elements exceed this card’s narrower entry-contract role. Ours reaches the assigned leaving state with comparable functional force and hands the reader cleanly to the next chapter’s definition of BAD SUGAR.\n\n**Largest momentum loss:** NONE"},"sequence_number":640}

